Sogen: floating-point exceptions reach the guest as Windows raises them

The x64 interrupt hook turned vectors 0, 1, 3, 6, 41 and 45 into Windows
exceptions and only logged the rest. So an x87 floating-point error (#MF,
16) or an SSE floating-point exception (#XM, 19) was logged ("Interrupt 16")
and the instruction resumed -- and an x87 exception stays pending until the
status word is cleared, so it was raised again at once, forever.

A runtime that unmasks floating-point traps meets it as soon as a float
overflows. SBCL does: it unmasks overflow, invalid and divide-by-zero in
the x87 control word and MXCSR, maps Windows' STATUS_FLOAT_* codes to Lisp
arithmetic errors, and its compiler handles those while folding constants.
Building nodecode under Sogen went silent seven minutes in and was killed at
the 90-minute limit; its log's last 20000 lines were all "Interrupt 16".

Both vectors now dispatch the exception Windows reports for the pending,
unmasked flags: invalid operation first (a stack check when the x87 stack
faulted), then divide by zero, denormal operand, overflow, underflow,
inexact result. For #MF the pending state goes with the CONTEXT just laid
and is cleared from the CPU, so the handler's own x87 code does not raise it
again; an SSE exception is precise and leaves nothing pending.

